'''Honda Shigemasa''' (本多重正, ''Honda Shigemasa'') (22 August 1605 – 15 September 1671) was the fourth [[Shogun|shōgun]] of the [[Honda Shogunate]], ruling from 1651 until his death in 1671. As the eldest son of the second shogun, [[Honda Shigetomo]], and the grandson of the shogunate's founder, [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada (Emperor Shirakawa)]], Shigemasa inherited a military government that had been stabilized by his uncle, the third shogun [[Honda Shigetoki]]. His twenty-year reign transformed the political, economic, and religious landscape of [[Mitoyagi]] (modern-day [[Okaiken]]) more profoundly than any of his predecessors.

Shigemasa is widely remembered as the most ruthless and authoritarian ruler in Sanese history. Abandoning the diplomatic clemency of his uncle, Shigemasa ruled through absolute terror, executing potential rivals and completely stripping the [[daimyō]] class of their remaining autonomy. His reign is defined by the implementation of draconian nationwide cadastral surveys, the brutal suppression of all non-Buddhist religions (particularly [[Christianity]]), and the enactment of the [[Kaikin|Great Seclusion (Kaikin)]] laws. These policies effectively sealed Mitoyagi's fate, locking the island nation into a deeply stagnant but unbroken two-hundred-year period of isolationist peace known as the '''Pax Honda'''.

Unlike his father, who died a tragic martyr seeking forgiveness, or his uncle, who ruled through patient bureaucratic consensus, Shigemasa governed through the cold calculus of fear. He believed, with the fervour of a man shaped by personal trauma, that mercy was the luxury of those who had never been betrayed — and that the only way to prevent another [[Kinzō Incident]] was to make the very thought of rebellion unthinkable.

== Early life ==

=== Birth and lineage ===

Shigemasa was born on 22 August 1605 in the new capital of [[Okami]], the eldest son of [[Honda Shigetomo]], the second Shogun, and his primary wife, [[Lady Saigō]] (西郷の方, ''Saigō-no-kata''), a daughter of the allied [[Saigō clan]]. The marriage between Shigetomo and Lady Saigō had been arranged in 1603 to cement the Honda clan's alliance with the Saigō during the final stages of the unification campaigns.

Through his paternal grandmother, [[Hatsu-no-kata]] (the daughter of the revered naval commander [[Anjō Motonobu]]), Shigemasa was the great-grandson of one of the Four Honda Heavenly Pillars. Through his paternal grandfather, [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada (Emperor Shirakawa)]], he was the direct descendant of the [[Imperial Court|imperial line]] — the grandson of a man who had been revealed, in a dramatic court ceremony in 1601, to be the lost Prince Fumihito, abducted as a child during the [[Yuzawa Incident]] of 1558 and raised in ignorance of his royal blood.

This dual heritage — martial and divine — placed an extraordinary weight on Shigemasa from birth. He was simultaneously the heir to the Shogunal line (the military-administrative branch of the Honda family) and a direct blood descendant of the Chrysanthemum Throne. The [[Dual Sovereignty|Edict of the Two Realms]] (両界の令, ''Ryōkai-no-Rei''), enacted by Shirakawa in 1611, had formally split the family into two branches: the Imperial Line, descended from Shigemasa's uncle [[Emperor Daigo|Prince Takahito (Emperor Daigo)]], and the Shogunal Line, descended from Shigetomo. As Shigetomo's eldest son, Shigemasa was groomed from infancy to inherit the military arm of the state.

=== Childhood in Okami ===

Shigemasa's early childhood was spent in the rapidly expanding capital of Okami, which his grandfather Shirakawa had transformed from a fishing village into a grand metropolis beginning in 1603. The boy grew up surrounded by the monumental construction projects of the ''Tenka-busin'' (public works), watching as defeated daimyō were forced to contribute stone and timber for the construction of [[Okami Castle]] — a daily, visible lesson in the power of the central government and the subjugation of the warlords.

His mother, Lady Saigō, was known as a patron of the arts and a devout [[Jōdo-shū]] Buddhist. She was described in contemporaneous accounts as having a calming influence on Shigetomo's often volatile temperament. Shigemasa's early years were shaped by her religious devotion and her cultural refinement, but also by the martial atmosphere of the shogunal court, where the veterans of the [[Battle of Tone]] and the [[Sengoku period]] still walked the corridors of power.

Shigemasa had three younger siblings: a sister, Chiyo (born 1607), who would later be married to [[Okudaira Tadamasa]] to secure the shogunate's inner circle of allies; a brother, Honda Tadamasa (born 1608), who would later be adopted by their uncle Shigetoki to ensure the continuation of that branch family; and an adopted brother, Honda Shigeie (born 1611), who was in fact the son of loyal retainer [[Hotta Muneshige]], adopted by Shigetomo to bind the Hotta clan permanently to the main Honda line.

=== Military education ===

During his youth, Shigemasa was groomed for supreme command, mirroring the harsh military education his father had received. Where Shigetomo had been trained as a battlefield vanguard — earning the terrifying moniker of "the Punisher" (お仕置き, ''Oshioki'') — Shigemasa's education was more structured, reflecting the institutionalization of the shogunate. He was trained in spear combat, horsemanship, and the use of firearms, but also in the bureaucratic arts of governance: cadastral surveying, tax assessment, legal code administration, and the management of the [[Okami Rice Exchange]].

His tutors included veterans of the Sengoku campaigns, men who had fought at Tone and survived the chaotic "Crimson Fog." From them, Shigemasa absorbed not only martial skills but also the deep-seated paranoia of the Sengoku generation — the belief that alliances were temporary, that loyalty was conditional, and that any display of weakness invited catastrophe.

Contemporaneous accounts describe the young Shigemasa as intelligent, observant, and unnervingly quiet. Unlike his father, whose youthful volatility was legendary, Shigemasa was noted for his stillness. He listened more than he spoke, and when he spoke, his words were precise and deliberate. This trait — the capacity for patient, calculating silence — would define his political style for the rest of his life.

=== Relationship with his father ===

The relationship between Shigemasa and his father Shigetomo was complex and, in many ways, defined by distance. Shigetomo, as Shogun, was consumed by the administrative burdens of statecraft — the implementation of the [[Kinzō reforms|Kinzō Reforms]], the construction of the [[Hokuto Omi - Shinshu road|Hokuto Omi–Shinshu road]], the management of the Okami Rice Exchange, and the delicate diplomacy of the Dual Sovereignty system. He was frequently absent from the family residence, travelling between Okami and the various provinces under his jurisdiction.

When Shigetomo was present, he was a transformed man. The volatile, bloodthirsty warlord of his youth had matured into a calm, pragmatic, and remarkably benevolent administrator — a shift heavily influenced by his wife Lady Saigō and his religious mentor, [[Shimotsuma Rairen]]. Shigemasa, however, never fully witnessed this transformation. He saw his father primarily through the lens of official duty: a distant, imposing figure who issued decrees and administered justice with the same cold efficiency he had once applied to battlefield executions.

The few intimate moments between father and son that survive in the historical record suggest a relationship marked by formality rather than warmth. Shigetomo, who had himself been raised as a hostage and trained for war from childhood, seems to have struggled to express affection. He expressed his love through preparation — ensuring that Shigemasa received the best possible education, the most capable tutors, and the most thorough grounding in the mechanics of power. But the emotional distance between them left a lasting mark on the young heir.

== The Kinzō Incident ==

{{Main|Kinzō Incident}}

Shigemasa's life was violently upended in June 1625, when he was just twenty years old. The [[Kinzō Incident]] — the coup d'état and assassination that claimed the lives of both his grandfather and his father within twelve days — was the defining trauma of his life, the event that shaped every political decision he would make for the next forty-six years.

=== The assassination of Emperor Shirakawa ===

On the night of 6 June 1625, a coalition of disgruntled vassals led by [[Ii Naotaka]] — the son of [[Ii Naomasa]], one of the Four Honda Heavenly Pillars and Shigetomo's childhood friend — launched a surprise assault on [[Toyozawa Castle]], the retirement palace of the Retired Emperor Shirakawa. Using a severe thunderstorm as cover, and aided by internal betrayal from the [[Ogasawara clan]], the Ii forces breached the castle walls.

The 69-year-old Shirakawa, awakened by the sound of musketry, donned his ceremonial armor and took up his legendary spear, the ''Tonbokiri''. According to the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'', the official chronicle of the Honda clan, he held the "Bridge of Heaven" — the wooden corridor connecting the inner palace to the keep — single-handedly for nearly an hour, slaying over thirty men. When Ii Naotaka pleaded with him to surrender, Shirakawa reportedly replied: "The wheel of time turns forward, Manchiyo. You cannot stop the sun from rising by shooting arrows at the sky. If you want the old world back, you must step over my corpse to get it."

Unwilling to engage his former lord in single combat, Naotaka ordered his arquebusiers to open fire. Shirakawa was struck by seven bullets and died standing, propped up by his spear.

=== The death of Shigetomo ===

{{Main|Great Reconciliation}}

The news reached Shigetomo at Okami Castle on 7 June — 38 hours after the attack began, delayed by the "Tyranny of Distance": the 500-kilometre gap between Okami and Toyozawa, compounded by the typhoon that had severed all naval communication. By the time the shogunal fleet reached Toyozawa on 10 June, Shirakawa had been dead for nearly 90 hours.

Shigetomo suffered a nervous breakdown upon discovering his father's body. The rational, reformist bureaucrat vanished, replaced by the erratic, vengeance-fuelled "Punisher" of his youth. He declared the Ii clan "Enemies of the State" (朝敵, ''Chōteki'') and mobilized the army for the [[Siege of Ichijoudani]].

On 18 June 1625, Shigetomo was mortally wounded by an Ii musketeer outside the gates of [[Ichijoudani Castle]]. He died in the mud alongside his childhood friend Ii Naotaka, who committed seppuku beside him in a pact of brotherhood. Shigetomo's final words — "Seal it" (封じろ, ''Fūjiro'') — would become one of the most famous phrases in Sanese history, the word that ended the Sengoku period.

=== Shigemasa's experience of the crisis ===

For the twenty-year-old Shigemasa, the Kinzō Incident was a cascade of catastrophes that unfolded faster than he could comprehend. He was in Okami when the first desperate messenger arrived — the "Red Letter" that read: "The Red Devils are inside the walls. We are holding the Keep." He watched his father collapse into grief and rage, witnessed the mobilization of the loyalist armies, and then received the news that Shigetomo had been killed at Ichijoudani.

In the span of twelve days, Shigemasa had lost his grandfather, his father, and his presumed birthright. The psychological impact was devastating and permanent. Contemporaneous accounts describe the young Shigemasa as being "frozen" during this period — he did not weep, did not rage, did not speak. He simply watched, with the same unnerving stillness that had defined his childhood, as the world he had been raised to inherit was dismantled around him.

=== Being bypassed for the succession ===

To prevent a nationwide civil war, the Imperial Court under [[Emperor Daigo]] bypassed the young Shigemasa and installed his uncle, [[Honda Shigetoki]], as the third Shogun on 10 July 1625. Shigetoki, the scholarly half-brother of Shigetomo (born to a low-ranking concubine named Lady Okyō), was a calm, competent bureaucrat — not a warrior. He had never been considered a candidate for the succession, and his elevation was a purely pragmatic decision: the nation needed a steady hand, not a grieving, untested twenty-year-old who might seek vengeance.

The decision was almost certainly correct. Shigetoki's first act was the [[Great Reconciliation|Edict of Clemency]], which pardoned the Ii clan and prevented a second Sengoku period. But for Shigemasa, the bypass was a profound personal wound — the second betrayal in a month, after the assassination of his grandfather. He had been raised to believe that he was the heir to the military legacy of the Honda clan, the son of the Punisher, the grandson of the Warrior who Surpassed Death. And now he was told to step aside while his uncle forgave the men who had killed his family.

== The twenty-six years of waiting ==

=== Resentment and the Edict of Clemency ===

{{Main|Great Reconciliation}}

Historical records indicate that Shigemasa deeply resented his uncle's pragmatic mercy. He viewed the [[Great Reconciliation]] — the political settlement that pardoned the Ii clan and allowed them to keep their territory in Ichijoudani, stripped of only 70,000 koku — as a profound insult to his father's memory and his grandfather's sacrifice.

The bloodstained letter that Shigetomo had carried to the gates of Ichijoudani — the letter of forgiveness that was the legal and moral foundation of the Edict of Clemency — was, in Shigemasa's eyes, a document born of his father's psychological collapse, not his true will. Shigemasa reportedly never accepted the narrative that Shigetomo had gone to the gates seeking reconciliation. He privately maintained, for the rest of his life, that his father had been murdered — that the shot was not a tragic accident but a deliberate assassination, and that the letter was written during a fleeting moment of sobriety that did not represent Shigetomo's true intentions.

This belief — that mercy had been extracted from weakness, that the Great Reconciliation was a capitulation dressed up as compassion — became the philosophical foundation of Shigemasa's entire political programme. He did not merely disagree with his uncle's policies; he believed they were existentially dangerous. Clemency, in Shigemasa's view, had not prevented civil war — it had merely delayed it, by allowing the enemies of the state to survive, regroup, and bide their time.

=== Building a network ===

For twenty-six years, from 1625 to 1651, Shigemasa lived in the shadow of his uncle's reign. He held no official position of supreme authority, though as the eldest son of the second Shogun, he maintained considerable prestige and a personal household within [[Okami Castle]]. He used this time not to sulk or to plot open rebellion — that would have been contrary to his nature — but to quietly build a network of fiercely loyal, hardline military retainers who shared his vision.

These men were drawn from a specific demographic: younger sons of fudai daimyō who had been marginalized by Shigetoki's bureaucratic, conciliatory governance; veterans of the Sengoku campaigns who chafed under the peacetime restrictions; and a new generation of administrators who believed that the shogunate's reliance on consensus and negotiation was a dangerous anachronism in a nation still bristling with armed warlords.

Shigemasa cultivated these relationships with the same patient, calculating stillness that had defined his childhood. He hosted private gatherings, sponsored martial arts tournaments, and quietly funded the training of select retainers. He studied the administrative records of the shogunate, identifying inefficiencies, vulnerabilities, and points of leverage. He mapped the personal networks of every major daimyō — their alliances, their grievances, their secrets — and filed this information away.

The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Shigemasa, during this period, never once openly challenged his uncle's authority. He attended court functions, performed his ceremonial duties, and maintained an impeccable facade of filial respect. But those who knew him closely — including his mother, Lady Saigō, who reportedly shared her son's reservations about the Edict of Clemency — understood that his silence was not acceptance. It was patience.

=== The influence of Lady Saigō ===

Shigemasa's mother, [[Lady Saigō]], played a significant role in shaping his political worldview during this period. As the widow of Shigetomo, she occupied a position of considerable influence within the shogunal household. While the historical record on Lady Saigō is still being compiled (her article is being written in parallel with this one), contemporaneous accounts describe her as a woman of deep intelligence, religious conviction, and quiet determination.

Lady Saigō had witnessed her husband's transformation from the "Punisher" to the reformist bureaucrat, and had been instrumental in that transformation. But after the Kinzō Incident, after watching her husband die in the mud of Ichijoudani, her perspective shifted. She did not become vengeful — her Jōdo-shū faith precluded that — but she came to believe, like her son, that the Edict of Clemency had been a mistake. She reportedly told Shigemasa, in a private conversation recorded by a household attendant: "Your father forgave because his heart broke. A broken heart is not a policy."

This phrase — "a broken heart is not a policy" — became a private motto for Shigemasa, encapsulating his conviction that governance must be guided by reason and calculation, not by emotion and sentiment.

== Ascension ==

=== The death of Shigetoki ===

[[Honda Shigetoki]] died peacefully of natural causes at [[Okami Castle]] on 12 November 1651, at the age of 68. His twenty-six-year reign had been characterized by bureaucratic consolidation, cultural patronage, and the careful maintenance of the peace established by the Great Reconciliation. He had slowed the aggressive implementation of the Kinzō Reforms, opting for a gradual transition of land rights that appeased the conservative samurai class. He had expanded the Okami Rice Exchange and subsidized the arts, leading to a flourishing of early modern Sanese culture.

Shigetoki's death was peaceful, but the transition that followed was anything but. The 46-year-old Shigemasa finally ascended as the fourth Shogun, and the political establishment that had grown comfortable under Shigetoki's gentle, consensus-driven rule was about to be violently disabused of its complacency.

=== The transfer of power ===

The formal investiture of Shigemasa as the fourth Shogun took place on 28 November 1651, overseen by [[Emperor Daigo]], who had by then been emperor for over three decades. The ceremony was conducted with full pomp and ceremony, but those who observed Shigemasa closely noted a chilling detail: unlike his father and uncle, who had received the shogunal commission with expressions of humility and gratitude, Shigemasa accepted the title with a flat, unreadable expression. He did not smile. He did not bow. He simply took the commission, turned to the assembled daimyō, and said, in a voice described by one witness as "cold enough to freeze the ink on the page": "The era of indulgence is over."

No one in the room doubted what he meant.

== The Keian Purges ==

=== Philosophy of terror ===

Shigemasa firmly believed that clemency bred weakness and that the only way to prevent another Kinzō Incident was through overwhelming fear. He had spent twenty-six years watching his uncle's conciliatory approach, and he had concluded that it had not pacified the nation — it had merely emboldened its enemies. The tozama daimyō who had been pardoned after the Kinzō Incident had not been grateful; they had been calculating, using the years of peace to rebuild their wealth, expand their private armies, and quietly test the limits of shogunal authority.

Within the first two years of his reign, Shigemasa moved to dismantle the conciliatory political structure his uncle had built. He did not act impulsively — true to his nature, he had spent decades mapping the networks, identifying the vulnerabilities, and planning the sequence. The result was the '''Keian Purges''' (慶安の粛清, ''Keian no Shukusei''), named after the [[Keian]] era (1648–1652) in which they began.

=== The role of the metsuke ===

The instrument of the purges was the ''[[metsuke]]'' (目付), the shogunate's secret police and intelligence apparatus. Originally established by Shirakawa as a network of inspectors and informants, the metsuke had been maintained but softened under Shigetoki's reign. Shigemasa transformed it into a weapon of terrifying efficiency.

He expanded the metsuke's mandate from passive surveillance to active investigation. He recruited from the ranks of disenfranchised rōnin, dispossessed samurai, and Buddhist clergy — men who had no clan ties, no local loyalties, and everything to gain from the shogunate's favour. These agents were deployed to every major domain in Mitoyagi, tasked with gathering intelligence on the daimyō's private armies, financial reserves, foreign contacts, and internal disputes.

The metsuke operated with a degree of autonomy that was unprecedented in Sanese history. They could arrest, interrogate, and detain without warrant. They reported directly to Shigemasa, bypassing the Council of Elders that had traditionally advised the Shogun. This effectively made the metsuke a parallel government — a shadow apparatus that answered only to the Shogun himself.

=== Manufacturing treason ===

Utilizing the metsuke's intelligence network, Shigemasa manufactured charges of treason against dozens of powerful tozama (outsider) and fudai (insider) daimyō who had shown independent ambitions during Shigetoki's lenient reign. The charges were varied but followed a predictable pattern: concealed weapons caches, unreported income from illegal trade, secret correspondence with foreign powers, or — the most damning charge of all — private expressions of sympathy for the Ii clan's grievances during the Kinzō Incident.

The beauty of the charges, from Shigemasa's perspective, was that they were often partially true. The daimyō of the early Honda period were former warlords, men who had spent their lives in the Sengoku period and who had not fully internalized the norms of peacetime governance. Many ''did'' maintain larger private armies than the shogunate officially permitted. Many ''did'' engage in off-the-books trade. Many ''did'' privately express resentment toward the central government. Shigemasa did not need to fabricate evidence from nothing — he simply needed to find the evidence that already existed and amplify it to the level of capital treason.

=== Stripping of lands and executions ===

Entire clans were summarily stripped of their lands (''[[kaieki]]'') or forced to commit seppuku. The first major target was the [[Ogasawara clan|Ogasawara]] cadet branch in Yagikoshu, which had never fully recovered from the disgrace of Ogasawara Tadayoji's betrayal during the Kinzō Incident. The Ogasawara domain was confiscated in its entirety, its ruling family reduced to rōnin status. This sent an immediate shockwave through the daimyō class: if the Ogasawara, who had been part of the shogunate's inner circle for decades, could be destroyed, no one was safe.

Over the next five years, the purges claimed an estimated thirty-seven daimyō families. Some were attainted for treason and executed; others were found guilty of lesser charges and stripped of their domains, their territories absorbed directly into the shogunate's holdings. The cumulative effect was the most massive concentration of power in the shogunate's history: by 1657, the direct holdings of the Honda Shogunate had increased by over 40%, while the tozama daimyō's collective territory had been reduced to its lowest point since the founding of the shogunate.

=== The Ii clan ===

The Ii clan, despite — or because of — their central role in the Kinzō Incident, was treated with a more complex strategy. Shigemasa did not move against them immediately, knowing that the bloodstained letter of forgiveness carried by Shigetomo at Ichijoudani made a direct assault on the Ii politically dangerous. The Ii clan's sacred treasure — the letter that proved the Shogun had forgiven them — was a powerful shield.

Instead, Shigemasa isolated them. He stationed metsuke agents throughout the Ii domain, monitored their every movement, and gradually restricted their political influence at court. He did not strip them of their territory, but he made it clear that the Ii were on probation — that their survival depended not on the letter of a dead man, but on the sufferance of the living Shogun.

The Ii clan, understanding the new reality, complied. They became model vassals, contributing generously to the shogunate's coffers and providing troops for the shogunate's campaigns. But the relationship was one of mutual suspicion, not the brotherhood that Shigetomo and Naotaka had sealed in the mud of Ichijoudani.

== The sankin-kōtai system ==

=== Institutionalizing hostage-taking ===

To ensure the physical subjugation of the lords, Shigemasa institutionalized the ''[[sankin-kōtai]]'' (参勤交代, "alternate attendance") system. While variations of the practice had existed under previous shoguns, Shigemasa transformed it from a customary obligation into a rigid, legally enforced system backed by severe penalties.

Under the Shōō Code of 1653, all daimyō were legally required to spend alternating years living in [[Okami]], in residences maintained at their own expense near the shogunal castle. While the daimyō was in residence, he was under constant surveillance by the metsuke. While the daimyō was in his home domain, his wife and heirs were required to remain in Okami as permanent hostages of the shogunate.

The system was designed to achieve multiple objectives simultaneously. First, it physically separated the daimyō from their power bases, making coordinated rebellion nearly impossible. Second, it forced the daimyō to maintain two households — one in their domain and one in Okami — at enormous expense, systematically draining their financial reserves and preventing them from accumulating wealth sufficient to fund private armies. Third, it created a community of daimyō in Okami who, despite their regional rivalries, were forced to interact, compete for shogunal favour, and absorb the culture of the capital — a process that gradually transformed them from independent warlords into dependent courtiers.

=== The processions ===

The sankin-kōtai processions themselves became major cultural events. Each daimyō was required to travel to and from Okami with a retinue proportional to his kokudaka — a practice that served as both a status display and a visible demonstration of the shogunate's control. The processions, which could involve hundreds or even thousands of retainers, marched along the great roads built by Shigetomo, turning the highways of Mitoyagi into constant, rolling displays of daimyō subservience.

The cost of these processions was staggering. A large tozama domain might spend 30–50% of its annual revenue on the alternate attendance system — on the maintenance of the Okami residence, the cost of the procession, and the living expenses of the hostage family. This was not a bug but a feature: Shigemasa designed the system to be financially devastating, ensuring that even the wealthiest daimyō could never accumulate the resources necessary to challenge the central government.

=== Enforcement ===

Any clan that dared usurp power, resist the mandates, or fail to comply with the sankin-kōtai requirements was met with immediate, merciless military annihilation. Shigemasa established a rapid-response military force — the ''Okami Hatamoto Guard'' — stationed at strategic points along the major roads, capable of reaching any domain in Mitoyagi within days. The message was unambiguous: compliance was not optional, and resistance was suicide.

== The Great Shōō Cadastral Surveys ==

=== Weaponizing the land survey ===

{{See also|Kinzō reforms}}

While his father Shigetomo had initiated the early [[Kinzō reforms|Kinzō Reforms]] to nationalize land surveying, and his uncle Shigetoki had continued those reforms at a cautious, gradual pace, it was Shigemasa who weaponized the system. In 1654, three years into his reign, he ordered the commencement of the '''Great Shōō Cadastral Surveys''' (正応地調査, ''Shōō Chichōsa''), named after the [[Shōō]] era (1651–1655).

Unlike previous surveys, which had relied partially on the self-reporting of local lords and allowed for negotiated adjustments, Shigemasa's surveys were absolute. He dispatched thousands of heavily armed shogunate officials — accompanied by metsuke agents and military escorts — to physically measure every rice paddy, field, fishery, and forest across the Sanese islands. The surveyors were equipped with standardized measuring rods, detailed maps, and instructions that left no room for interpretation.

=== The methodology ===

The surveys were conducted with a scientific rigor that was unprecedented in Sanese history. Every parcel of arable land was measured, classified by soil quality, and assigned a standardized kokudaka (rice yield) value. The surveyors assessed not only rice paddies but dry fields, orchards, fishing grounds, salt pans, and forest resources. They recorded the names of the cultivators, the boundaries of the plots, and the water rights associated with each field.

The data was compiled into massive registers — the ''Shōō Land Cadastre'' — which became the definitive record of the nation's productive capacity. These registers were stored in Okami, under the direct control of the shogunate, and were updated every six years through follow-up surveys. Any discrepancy between the registered figures and the actual production was treated as evidence of fraud, with severe consequences for the responsible officials.

=== Brutality of enforcement ===

The surveys were notoriously exacting and unforgiving. Peasants and local administrators caught hiding arable land from the inspectors were frequently executed on the spot — a practice that Shigemasa not only condoned but explicitly ordered. In one documented incident in [[Ise Province]], a village headman who had concealed three acres of newly cleared forest land was beheaded in the village square, and his head was displayed on a pole for three days as a warning.

The metsuke accompanied the survey teams not merely as observers but as interrogators. They questioned local officials about the financial practices of their daimyō, looking for evidence of underreporting, tax evasion, or the maintenance of unregistered military forces. The surveys were, in effect, a nationwide audit conducted at spear-point — simultaneously a revenue assessment and an intelligence operation.

=== Economic consequences ===

By standardizing the kokudaka system with terrifying precision, Shigemasa maximized the tax revenue flowing directly into Okami. The surveys revealed that many daimyō had been underreporting their productive capacity by 20–40%, concealing the surplus for their own use. The new, accurate assessments stripped this surplus overnight, redirecting it to the shogunal treasury.

This absolute economic stranglehold impoverished the regional daimyō, ensuring they could never afford to fund private armies or purchase foreign firearms to challenge the central government. The daimyō's financial records were now transparent to the shogunate, their tax obligations fixed and non-negotiable, their ability to accumulate wealth systematically constrained by the sankin-kōtai system and the cadastral surveys working in tandem.

The surveys also had a profound effect on the peasantry. While the standardization of tax rates eliminated some of the arbitrary exactions that had plagued farmers under self-reporting daimyō, the ruthlessness of the surveyors and the severity of the penalties for concealment created an atmosphere of pervasive fear. Peasants who had previously supplemented their income through unregistered plots of land found themselves subject to taxation on every square metre of soil, with no allowance for local variation in growing conditions.

== Religious suppression ==

=== The ideological threat ===

Shigemasa viewed any ideology that superseded absolute loyalty to the Shogun as a direct threat to the state. This conviction was not merely political but personal: his grandfather, Emperor Shirakawa, had been killed by men who believed that traditional warrior values took precedence over reformist governance. His father had been killed by a man who believed that the old order was worth dying for. In Shigemasa's calculus, ideology — religious, philosophical, or political — was the engine of rebellion.

Building upon his grandfather's initial expulsion edicts of 1614, Shigemasa launched a systematic and bloody campaign to eradicate all foreign and non-conformist faiths. The campaign was comprehensive, targeting not only Christianity but any religious practice that could not be brought under the direct supervision of the shogunate.

=== The suppression of Christianity ===

{{See also|Christianity in Okaiken}}

Shigemasa specifically targeted [[Christianity]], which had survived underground in regions like [[Ishijima]] following the expulsion edicts of 1614. The Christian community in Mitoyagi, though diminished, had maintained a fragile existence through secret worship, coded language, and the protection of sympathetic daimyō who had nominally converted but retained private sympathies.

Shigemasa escalated the use of ''[[fumi-e]]'' (踏み絵, "stepping pictures") — the practice of trampling on holy images — from a sporadic test into a mandatory annual requirement for every citizen. The practice was simple but devastating: officials would place a bronze image of Christ or the Virgin Mary on the ground, and every resident of a district was required to step on it. Those who refused, or who hesitated, were immediately arrested as suspected Christians.

Those who refused to trample the images were subjected to horrific public tortures. The methods were deliberately varied and escalating: some were hung upside down over pits of rotting filth until they recanted or died; others were subjected to crucifixion; the most notorious method was boiling at the hot springs of [[Mount Unzen]], where suspected Christians were lowered into the scalding mineral waters until their flesh peeled from their bones. These executions were public, deliberate, and intended as spectacle — a message to anyone who might consider embracing a foreign god.

The campaign was devastatingly effective. Within a decade of Shigemasa's ascension, the organized Christian community in Mitoyagi had been effectively destroyed. Surviving converts either apostatized, fled into the most remote mountain regions, or were executed. The few who maintained their faith did so in absolute secrecy, passing their beliefs down through generations in coded forms that would not be fully understood until the modern era.

=== The Danka system ===

To enforce ideological homogeneity and prevent the re-emergence of hidden Christian communities, Shigemasa legally mandated the '''Danka system''' (檀家制度, ''Danka seido''). Under this system, every citizen of Mitoyagi — from the highest samurai to the lowest peasant — was required to register with a local [[Jōdo-shū]] Buddhist temple. The registration was not optional; it was a legal prerequisite for citizenship, land tenure, and social standing.

Each temple was assigned a geographic territory, and every household within that territory was required to affiliate with the temple and participate in its rituals. The temple maintained a registry of all affiliated households — the ''Danka register'' (檀家帳, ''Dankachō'') — which was periodically inspected by shogunate officials. Any household that failed to maintain its temple affiliation was subject to investigation, and its members were presumed to be hidden Christians until proven otherwise.

The Buddhist clergy were effectively transformed into an arm of the shogunate's intelligence network. Temple priests were required to report any signs of religious dissent, unusual behaviour, or suspected Christian activity among their parishioners to the metsuke. The priests, in turn, were monitored by the metsuke — creating a system of mutual surveillance that extended from the highest levels of the shogunate to the smallest village temple.

By tying citizenship and social standing strictly to Buddhist temple registration, Shigemasa successfully eradicated competing religious influence. The Danka system would remain in place for over two centuries, becoming one of the most enduring legacies of his reign. It also had the unintended consequence of strengthening the institutional power of the Buddhist establishment, which became deeply intertwined with the shogunate's apparatus of control — a symbiosis that would not be fully unwound until the modern era.

=== Suppression of other faiths ===

While Christianity was the primary target, Shigemasa's religious policies extended to other non-conformist practices. The [[Ikkō-ikki]] tradition of militant religious rebellion, which had terrorized the Sengoku period, was systematically suppressed. Independent temples that maintained their own military forces were disarmed and brought under shogunal control. The [[Shugendō]] mountain ascetic tradition was restricted, its practitioners forced to affiliate with established Buddhist sects.

Even within the Buddhist establishment, Shigemasa imposed strict controls. He issued regulations governing temple hierarchies, ordination practices, and doctrinal disputes, effectively bringing the entire Buddhist institution under the supervision of the state. The era of independent religious power — the warrior-monks who had once defied emperors and shoguns alike — was over.

== The Kaikin (Closed Country) edicts ==

=== The fear of foreign influence ===

Shigemasa's most enduring legacy was his decision to sever Mitoyagi from the rest of the world. His grandfather Shirakawa had been a pragmatist regarding trade, issuing Vermilion Seal Permits (''Shu-in jō'') to authorized merchant ships and maintaining diplomatic and commercial relations with foreign powers, including the [[Volisanian Empire]] and the [[Zong|Kingdom of Zong]]. Shigetomo had continued this policy, even hosting the Volisanian Governor-General [[Ernesto Lacuña]] as an honoured guest.

Shigemasa, however, viewed foreign contact through the lens of security, not commerce. He feared that foreign powers would arm domestic rebels, that foreign missionaries would subvert the population, and that foreign ideas would dilute the absolute loyalty to the Shogun that he was working so hard to establish. The Christian rebellion that had simmered in Ishijima was, in Shigemasa's mind, proof that foreign religion was merely the precursor to foreign domination.

=== The edicts ===

Between 1655 and 1659, Shigemasa enacted a series of edicts collectively known as the '''Kaikin''' (海禁, "Maritime Prohibitions") or the '''Closed Country''' laws. These edicts were comprehensive and absolute:

* '''Prohibition of departure''': It became a capital offense for any Sanese citizen to leave the country. Any person who attempted to build or operate a vessel capable of overseas travel was subject to execution.
* '''Prohibition of return''': Any Sanese person who had previously lived abroad was forbidden from returning to Mitoyagi, effectively stranding thousands of merchants, sailors, and diplomats who had been conducting business in foreign ports. Those who attempted to return were executed upon arrival.
* '''Destruction of ocean-going vessels''': The construction of ocean-going vessels with keels was strictly outlawed. Existing ships were dismantled or burned. The once-proud [[Anjō-Murakami Suigun]] — the naval force that had helped Shigemasa's great-grandfather Anjō Motonobu secure control of the seas during the unification campaigns — was relegated to a coastal defense and policing force, stripped of its deep-water capabilities.
* '''Restriction of foreign trade''': [[Volisanian]] and [[Zong|Zongese]] merchants were banned from all mainland ports. Foreign trade was violently curtailed and channeled through a single, controlled point of entry.
* '''The Yokosuka window''': A singular, heavily guarded artificial island was constructed off the coast of [[Yokosuka]], the port city that Shigemasa's grandfather had established as his base during the northern campaigns. A severely limited number of foreign vessels — primarily Zongese and Volisanian — were permitted to dock at this island once a year, under the watchful eyes of the shogunate's military. All trade was conducted through shogunate-appointed brokers, with prices, quantities, and commodity types strictly regulated by the central government.

=== The rationale ===

Shigemasa's rationale for the Kaikin was articulated in a series of memoranda preserved in the shogunal archives. The core argument was strategic: foreign trade, while economically beneficial, created vulnerabilities that could be exploited by domestic enemies. Foreign merchants brought not only goods but ideas, weapons, and intelligence. Foreign missionaries brought a competing source of authority — a God who commanded loyalty above any earthly ruler. The only way to ensure the absolute loyalty of the population was to ensure that the Shogun was the only source of authority they could access.

The Kaikin was also an economic strategy. By monopolizing foreign trade through the Yokosuka window, the shogunate could control the flow of precious metals, luxury goods, and — most importantly — firearms. No daimyō, no matter how wealthy, could acquire foreign weapons without the shogunate's knowledge and consent. This effectively gave Shigemasa a monopoly on military technology, making armed rebellion not merely difficult but technologically impossible.

=== Consequences ===

The Kaikin sealed Mitoyagi into a technological and cultural time capsule. For over two centuries, the nation would develop in isolation, its internal culture, literature, and domestic agriculture flourishing without the constant threat of foreign invasion or domestic civil war. The Pax Honda — the two-hundred-year span of unbroken peace — was the direct result of Shigemasa's isolationist policies.

But the cost was immense. While the rest of the world underwent the Scientific Revolution, the Enlightenment, and the Industrial Revolution, Mitoyagi stagnated. The technological gap that opened during the period of isolation would, when the country was finally reopened in the 19th century, prove catastrophic — leaving the nation vulnerable to the very foreign powers that Shigemasa had sought to exclude.

== Governance and administration ==

=== The Shōō Code ===

In addition to the sankin-kōtai system and the cadastral surveys, Shigemasa codified his vision of governance in the '''Shōō Code''' (正応御定書, ''Shōō Osadamegaki''), a comprehensive legal framework promulgated in 1653 and expanded throughout his reign. The code covered virtually every aspect of governance: the obligations of daimyō, the regulation of the samurai class, the administration of justice, the management of temples and shrines, the conduct of foreign trade, and the regulation of daily life.

The Shōō Code was notable for its severity. Crimes that had previously been punished by exile or fine were now punishable by death. The code mandated collective responsibility — the family of a criminal was subject to punishment alongside the offender, ensuring that the threat of consequences extended beyond the individual to the entire kinship network. This principle of collective guilt was a direct application of Shigemasa's philosophy of fear: if a man knew that his rebellion would result not only in his own death but in the destruction of his entire family, he would think twice before drawing his sword.

=== Centralization of the bureaucracy ===

Shigemasa restructured the shogunal bureaucracy to concentrate power in his own hands. He reduced the authority of the Council of Elders (''Rōjū''), the advisory body that had wielded significant influence under Shigetoki, and elevated the metsuke to a position of primacy within the administrative hierarchy. He created new offices — the ''Gundai'' (intendants) who administered the shogunate's direct holdings, and the ''Kanjō-bugyō'' (finance commissioners) who oversaw the cadastral surveys and tax collection — and staffed them with men personally loyal to him, not to the great clans.

The result was a government that was, for the first time in Sanese history, truly centralized. The daimyō were reduced to the status of administrators, their domains managed according to shogunal regulations, their finances monitored by shogunal auditors, their movements controlled by the sankin-kōtai system. The shogunate was no longer a federation of semi-autonomous warlords held together by personal loyalty — it was a state.

== Relationship with his sons ==

=== Honda Shigenori ===

Shigemasa had two sons who survived to adulthood: '''Honda Shigenori''' (本多重則), his eldest son and designated heir, and '''Honda Masatada''' (本多正忠), his second son.

Shigemasa's relationship with Shigenori was defined by the same cold, calculating discipline that characterized his governance. He raised his heir as he had been raised — for command, not for affection. Shigenori was subjected to a rigorous education in military arts, administration, and the philosophy of rule through fear. Shigemasa personally supervised his son's training, drilling him in the principles of the Shōō Code and the rationale behind the Kaikin.

Contemporaneous accounts suggest that Shigemasa viewed Shigenori with a mixture of pride and suspicion. He was proud of his son's competence but wary of the possibility that Shigenori might, like his uncle Shigetoki, develop a preference for conciliation over coercion. To prevent this, Shigemasa deliberately exposed Shigenori to the harsh realities of his rule — requiring him to witness executions, participate in the cadastral surveys, and oversee the interrogation of suspected Christians.

The strategy was partially successful. Shigenori maintained his father's strict legal codes and isolationist policies after his death, ensuring the continuation of the Pax Honda. But he also tempered the worst excesses of Shigemasa's reign, reducing the frequency of executions and relaxing some of the more draconian provisions of the Shōō Code. He was, in many ways, the Shigetoki to Shigemasa's Shigetomo — the conciliator who followed the enforcer.

=== Honda Masatada ===

Shigemasa's second son, Masatada, was given a different role. Rather than being groomed for the shogunate, Masatada was designated as the heir to the cadet branch of the Honda family, responsible for maintaining the clan's military traditions and serving as a senior commander in the shogunal army. The relationship between Shigemasa and Masatada was less fraught than that with Shigenori, as Masatada's position did not carry the weight of succession.

Masatada was described as a capable soldier and a loyal retainer, but he lacked his father's political acumen. He served faithfully throughout Shigemasa's reign and continued to serve under Shigenori, maintaining the military readiness of the shogunate's forces without challenging the political direction set by his elder brother.

== Death ==

Shigemasa ruled with an iron fist until his death from [[stomach cancer]] on 15 September 1671, at the age of 66. The illness had been developing for at least two years before his death, and Shigemasa, characteristically, concealed his condition from all but his closest attendants. He continued to conduct the daily business of the shogunate, issue decrees, and oversee the administration of the empire even as the disease consumed him from within.

The ''Mitsukoji'' records that in his final weeks, as the pain became unbearable, Shigemasa refused all medication that would dull his mind. He insisted on remaining lucid until the end, personally drafting his final directives to the Council of Elders and his son Shigenori. His last recorded words, spoken to his heir, were: "Do not forgive. Forgiveness is the luxury of those who can afford to be betrayed. We cannot."

He died in his private chambers at [[Okami Castle]], attended only by his personal physician and a single metsuke agent who recorded the time of death. His body was interred at the Honda family temple in Okami, in a ceremony that was — by Shigemasa's own instruction — austere and private, a stark contrast to the elaborate state funerals of his predecessors.

He was succeeded by his son, [[Honda Shigenori]], who maintained the strict legal codes and isolationist policies established by his father, ensuring the continuation of the Pax Honda for another century and a half.

== Legacy ==

=== The polarizing figure ===

History views Honda Shigemasa as a highly polarizing figure — perhaps the most polarizing in Sanese historiography. The debate over his legacy has raged for over three centuries and shows no sign of resolution.

==== The tyrant thesis ====

Modern democratic scholars and revisionist historians often condemn Shigemasa as a paranoid tyrant who plunged Okaiken into a dark age of oppressive censorship, technological stagnation, and cultural isolation. They point to the Keian Purges as a campaign of political terror comparable to the worst excesses of any totalitarian state; to the cadastral surveys as an instrument of economic exploitation that impoverished both the daimyō and the peasantry; to the religious suppression as a campaign of genocide against an entire faith community; and to the Kaikin as a catastrophic misjudgment that severed Mitoyagi from the trajectory of human progress.

Under this interpretation, Shigemasa was not a unifier but a strangler — a man whose personal trauma was transmuted into state policy, whose fear of betrayal became the operating principle of an entire nation, and whose inability to distinguish between mercy and weakness led him to construct a system so rigid, so repressive, and so isolated that it was destined to collapse under the weight of its own stagnation.

The tyrant thesis emphasizes the human cost of Shigemasa's policies: the thousands executed in the Keian Purges, the hundreds tortured and killed for their religious beliefs, the generations of peasants ground down by the cadastral surveys, the merchants and sailors stranded abroad by the Kaikin, and the entire nation locked in a technological time capsule from which it would take a violent and humiliating awakening to escape.

==== The necessary unifier thesis ====

Traditionalist historians argue that Shigemasa's ruthless centralization was a grim necessity. They point to the context: a nation that had endured 158 years of civil war, that had lost its founding emperor to assassination, that had nearly collapsed into a second Sengoku period, and that was surrounded by daimyō who still possessed the resources, the weapons, and the will to resume the fighting at the first sign of weakness.

Under this interpretation, the Keian Purges were not gratuitous cruelty but a surgical excision of the warlord class — the final, necessary step in the transition from a federation of semi-autonomous domains to a centralized state. The cadastral surveys were not exploitation but modernization — the creation of a rational, standardized tax system that replaced the chaotic, self-reported assessments of the Sengoku era. The religious suppression was not genocide but national security — the elimination of a foreign ideological vector that had already proven its capacity to inspire rebellion. And the Kaikin was not isolation but preservation — the deliberate choice to protect the nation's sovereignty and cultural identity from the depredations of colonial powers that were, in the same period, subjugating much of the known world.

The necessary unifier thesis emphasizes the outcome: by breaking the spirit of the warlords and sealing the borders, Shigemasa engineered the Pax Honda — a two-hundred-year span devoid of major warfare, allowing internal culture, literature, and domestic agriculture to flourish without the constant threat of the Sengoku bloodshed his ancestors had endured. The population of Mitoyagi doubled during the Pax Honda. The arts — poetry, theatre, woodblock printing, the novel — reached levels of sophistication that would not have been possible in a nation perpetually at war. The urban culture of Okami became one of the most vibrant in the world, a city of a million people sustained by the peace that Shigemasa's terror had purchased.

==== The synthesis ====

A growing school of modern historians, particularly at the [[University of Okami]] and [[Toho Shuheisha University]], has attempted to move beyond the binary of tyrant versus unifier. They argue that Shigemasa was both — that his policies were simultaneously brutal and effective, that his terror was real and his peace was real, and that the two cannot be separated. The Pax Honda was not built on consensus or shared values but on fear — and the fact that it lasted two centuries does not vindicate the fear, but it does complicate the condemnation.

This synthesis views Shigemasa as a man defined by trauma — the twenty-year-old who watched his world collapse in twelve days, who was told to step aside while his uncle forgave the men who killed his family, and who spent the next forty-six years ensuring that no one would ever be in a position to do to his successors what the Ii clan had done to his grandfather. He was not evil, nor was he good. He was the product of a specific history, and his policies were the expression of a specific fear.

=== The Pax Honda ===

{{Main|Pax Honda}}

The '''Pax Honda''' (パクス・ホンダ, ''Pakusu Honda'') — the 260-year period of relative peace that followed the Sengoku period — is Shigemasa's most enduring legacy, though it was founded by the Great Reconciliation and merely enforced by his policies. The Pax Honda was not merely an absence of war; it was a comprehensive system of social, economic, and political control that maintained peace through the systematic prevention of any challenge to the central authority.

Under the Pax Honda, the daimyō were reduced to administrators, the samurai class was transformed from warriors into bureaucrats, the peasantry was tied to the land through the cadastral system, and the entire population was monitored through the Danka system. Foreign contact was eliminated, religious dissent was eradicated, and the shogunate's authority was made absolute.

The Pax Honda ended only with the [[Saito Restoration]] of 1870, when the last shogun, [[Honda Yoshinobu]], voluntarily returned administrative power to the emperor. By that time, the technological gap between Mitoyagi and the rest of the world had become so vast that the nation was forced into a traumatic and rapid modernization programme — the very outcome that Shigemasa's isolationist policies had been designed to prevent, albeit in a different form.

=== Comparison with his predecessors ===

Shigemasa's reign is often compared to those of his father and uncle, and the contrast is instructive. Shigetomo, the second Shogun, was a man of contradictions — a battlefield terror who became a reformist bureaucrat, a man of violence who died seeking forgiveness. Shigetoki, the third Shogun, was a scholar and a conciliator, a man who ruled through patience and consensus. Shigemasa, the fourth Shogun, was neither: he was not a man of violence who sought redemption, nor a scholar who sought consensus. He was a calculator — a man who ruled through the systematic application of fear, who treated governance as an engineering problem to be solved through the precise calibration of incentives and punishments.

Where Shigetomo had been driven by emotion — rage, grief, love, remorse — Shigemasa was driven by analysis. Where Shigetoki had been guided by principle — mercy, consensus, gradualism — Shigemasa was guided by outcomes. He did not ask whether a policy was just; he asked whether it worked. He did not ask whether a punishment was proportionate; he asked whether it was sufficient to deter. This instrumental, utilitarian approach to governance was, in many ways, more modern than that of his predecessors — and more disturbing.

== In popular culture ==

Due to his reputation as a tyrant, Shigemasa is almost exclusively portrayed as an antagonist or a terrifying, Machiavellian anti-hero in Sanese media. His cold, calculating demeanour and his willingness to sacrifice individual lives for the sake of systemic stability make him a natural villain for dramatic narratives, while the psychological depth of his trauma — the young man who watched his world collapse and spent his life ensuring it could never happen again — provides material for more nuanced portrayals.

=== Television ===

In the iconic KTK [[Taisen Drama]] ''[[Honda Shigetomo (1983 TV series)|Honda Shigetomo]]'' (1983), the final episodes depict a young Shigemasa seething with quiet rage at his uncle's clemency. The portrayal, by actor [[Takeshi Minami]], emphasizes the contrast between Shigemasa's stillness and the emotional turbulence of those around him — a man who does not react because he is already calculating his next move. The series' final scene shows Shigemasa alone in a corridor of Okami Castle, watching his uncle's funeral procession with an expression of unreadable intensity, as the narrator intones: "The era of mercy was ending. The era of iron was about to begin."

Shigemasa is the central figure in the 2024 KTK series '''''Power to Live''''' (生きる力, ''Ikiru Chikara''), which controversially explores the psychological toll of enforcing the closed-country policies and the brutal purges required to maintain total peace. The series, created by [[Naoki Hashimoto]], depicts Shigemasa as a man trapped by his own system — a ruler who builds a prison for his nation and discovers, too late, that he has imprisoned himself. The portrayal, by actor [[Kenji Aoyama]], has been praised for its psychological complexity but criticized by traditionalist groups for humanizing a figure they consider beyond redemption.

He also appears in the 2008 series '''''Genna Revolt''''', which focuses on the political challenges of transitioning from a wartime to a peacetime government, where a young Shigemasa is shown observing the political machinations of his uncle's court with barely concealed contempt.

=== Literature ===

In the novel '''''The Middle Way''''' by [[Tsuji Kunio]], Shigemasa appears as a secondary character — a brooding presence in the background of the Great Reconciliation, whose silence is more threatening than the rage of the other characters. The novel's depiction of Shigemasa watching his uncle pardon the Ii clan, and then quietly turning away, has become one of the most iconic images in Sanese historical fiction.

In the novel '''''The Iron Shadow''''' (鉄の影, ''Tetsu no Kage'') by [[Yoko Mishima]], Shigemasa is the protagonist — a revisionist portrayal that attempts to reconstruct his inner life, depicting him as a man who genuinely believed that his cruelty was the only alternative to civil war. The novel was controversial upon publication, with critics accusing Mishima of apologism for tyranny, but it has since been recognized as one of the most psychologically complex portrayals of the Shogun in modern literature.

=== Video games ===

Historical representations in video games include ''Shogun: Total War'', ''Total War: Shogun 2'', and the ''Terumasa'' series, where Shigemasa typically appears as a late-game antagonist — the ruler whose policies of isolation and repression the player must either uphold or overthrow. In ''Age of Empires II: The Conquerors'', the Shigemasa campaign depicts the Keian Purges and the implementation of the Kaikin from a strategic perspective, allowing players to experience the logic — if not the morality — of his decisions.

